        "advisory": {
          "id": "RUSTSEC-2026-0343",
          "package": "quinn-proto",
          "title": "Outgoing datagram buffer accounting can panic the sender in quinn-proto",
          "description": "The limit on memory used to buffer outgoing datagrams, introduced in 0.11.17, subtracted an\nevicted datagram's size from the buffered byte counter twice when `Datagrams::send` was called\nwith `drop = true` (as `Connection::send_datagram` in the `quinn` crate does) while the buffer was\nfull. Under sustained eviction the counter drifts below its true value: debug builds panic with an\ninteger underflow, while release builds wrap the counter, drain the whole queue, and then panic on\na desynchronization check in a later `send` call. In `quinn`, this panic occurs while the\nconnection lock is held, poisoning it and causing a double panic (and thus an abort) when the\nconnection is dropped.\n\nThis can be triggered without a malicious peer, by an application that sends a burst of datagrams\nand then idles. It can also be triggered by a peer that keeps the connection alive with PING frames\nwhile withholding acknowledgements, so that the sender's congestion window stays closed and the\noutgoing queue never drains. Applications that use `send_datagram_wait` are not affected.\n\nOnly 0.11.17 is affected; the flaw was corrected in 0.11.18 by removing the redundant subtraction.\n\nThanks to [@alexpyattaev](https://github.com/alexpyattaev) for reporting and\n[@vcarus](https://github.com/vcarus) for fixing this issue.",
          "date": "2026-09-14",
          "aliases": [
            "GHSA-ppcp-v39w-8jq2"
          ],
