      {
        "advisory": {
          "id": "RUSTSEC-2026-0340",
          "package": "quinn-proto",
          "title": "Peer minimum ACK delay can panic quinn-proto when ACK_FREQUENCY transmission is enabled",
          "description": "When sending ACK_FREQUENCY frames is enabled through\n`TransportConfig::ack_frequency_config(Some(..))`, the peer's transport parameters could cause\n`AckFrequencyState::candidate_max_ack_delay` to call `Duration::clamp` with a minimum greater\nthan its maximum, which panics.\n\nFor example, a peer advertising a `min_ack_delay` of 100,000 microseconds and a `max_ack_delay`\nof 100 milliseconds passes transport parameter validation. At a 5 ms RTT, the lower bound of the\nclamp becomes 100 ms while the upper bound is 25 ms, violating the precondition of `clamp` and\npanicking while driving the connection. Both automatically derived and explicitly configured\nmaximum ACK delays use this calculation.\n\nSending ACK_FREQUENCY frames is disabled by default, so only endpoints that opt in are affected.\n\nThe flaw was corrected in 0.11.18 by computing the delay with explicit minimum and maximum bounds\ninstead of `clamp`.\n\nThanks to [@zerox80](https://github.com/zerox80) for reporting this issue.",
          "date": "2026-09-14",
